content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"(iii-2) µ f 2 E [ ‖ ˆ x η -x ∗ ‖ 2 ] -2 ‖∇ f ( x ∗ ) ‖ α Err η ≤ E [ f (ˆ x η )] -f ∗ ≤ 1 η Err η .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":547.4138006000001,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.662,"r":389.71182096999996,"t":565.0155557},"charspan":[0,99],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/71","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","enumerated":false,"label":"list_item","marker":"","orig":"(iii-3) If µ f > 0 , then E [ ‖ ˆ x η -x ∗ ‖ 2 ] ≤ 2 ηµ f Err η .","parent":{"$ref":"#/groups/1"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":530.6727262893372,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.66199999999998,"r":303.06354798,"t":549.2765557},"charspan":[0,65],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/72","text":"(iii-3) If µ f > 0 , then E [ ‖ ˆ x η -x ∗ ‖ 2 ] ≤ 2 ηµ f Err η ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Proof. (i-1) In view of the definition of Err η and f η , we have E [ h (ˆ x η ) + ηf (ˆ x η )] -f ∗ η ≤ Err η . Also, from the definition of f ∗ η , we have f ∗ η ≤ f η ( x ∗ ) = h ∗ + ηf ∗ . From the preceding two relations, we obtain","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":473.737361,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":541.51710202272,"t":516.2945557},"charspan":[0,236],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/73","text":"Proof. (i-1) In view of the definition of Err η and f η , we have E [ h (ˆ x η ) + ηf (ˆ x η )] -f ∗ η ≤ Err η . Also, from the definition of f ∗ η , we have f ∗ η ≤ f η ( x ∗ ) = h ∗ + ηf ∗ . From the preceding two relations, we obtain"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"E [ h (ˆ x η )] -h ∗ + η ( E [ f (ˆ x η )] -f ∗ ) ≤ Err η . (2)","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":449.68836100000004,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":208.284,"r":541.27664798,"t":465.1465557},"charspan":[0,63],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/74","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"By invoking Assumption 1, we have E [ f (ˆ x η )] > -∞ . Therefore, there exists some M > 0 such that f ∗ -E [ f (ˆ x η )] < M . As a result, we obtain E [ h (ˆ x η )] -h ∗ ≤ Err η + ηM . From ˆ x η ∈ X , we also have E [ h (ˆ x η )] -h ∗ ≥ 0. This completes the proof of (i-1).","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":398.540361,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":540.2989718918401,"t":441.0975557},"charspan":[0,278],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/75","text":"By invoking Assumption 1, we have E [ f (ˆ x η )] > -∞ . Therefore, there exists some M > 0 such that f ∗ -E [ f (ˆ x η )] < M . As a result, we obtain E [ h (ˆ x η )] -h ∗ ≤ Err η + ηM . From ˆ x η ∈ X , we also have E [ h (ˆ x η )] -h ∗ ≥ 0. This completes the proof of (i-1)."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(i-2) The upper bound holds in view of (2) and that E [ h (ˆ x η )] -h ∗ ≥ 0. To show the lower bound, from the convexity of f , we may write","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":371.442361,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.724,"r":541.5196503624198,"t":400.44955569999996},"charspan":[0,141],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/76","text":"(i-2) The upper bound holds in view of (2) and that E [ h (ˆ x η )] -h ∗ ≥ 0. To show the lower bound, from the convexity of f , we may write"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"µ f 2 E [ ‖ ˆ x η -x ∗ ‖ 2 ] + ∇ f ( x ∗ ) ⊤ E [(ˆ x η -x ∗ )] ≤ E [ f (ˆ x η )] -f ∗ ,","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":345.24880060000004,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":171.401,"r":441.79454798000006,"t":362.85055570000003},"charspan":[0,87],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/77","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"where the expectation is taken with respect to the random variables generated in the method M . Note that ∇ f ( x ∗ ) ⊤ E [(ˆ x η -x ∗ )] is not necessarily nonnegative. We may write","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":309.79436100000004,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":71.607,"r":542.1168534598,"t":338.8015557},"charspan":[0,182],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/78","text":"where the expectation is taken with respect to the random variables generated in the method M . Note that ∇ f ( x ∗ ) ⊤ E [(ˆ x η -x ∗ )] is not necessarily nonnegative. We may write"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"µ f 2 E [ ‖ ˆ x η -x ∗ ‖ 2 ] + ∇ f ( x ∗ ) ⊤ E [(ˆ x η -Π X ∗ h [ˆ x η ] + Π X ∗ h [ˆ x η ] -x ∗ )] ≤ E [ f (ˆ x η )] -f ∗ .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":282.9987262893371,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":120.101,"r":493.09454798,"t":301.2025557},"charspan":[0,124],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/79","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"In view of Π X ∗ h [ˆ x η ] ∈ X ∗ h , we have ∇ f ( x ∗ ) ⊤ E [( Π X ∗ h [ˆ x η ] -x ∗ )] ≥ 0. We obtain","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":255.03972628933695,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":448.7953201999999,"t":273.2435556999999},"charspan":[0,104],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/80","text":"In view of Π X ∗ h [ˆ x η ] ∈ X ∗ h , we have ∇ f ( x ∗ ) ⊤ E [( Π X ∗ h [ˆ x η ] -x ∗ )] ≥ 0. We obtain"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"µ f 2 E [ ‖ ˆ x η -x ∗ ‖ 2 ] + ∇ f ( x ∗ ) ⊤ E [( ˆ x η -Π X ∗ h [ˆ x η ] )] ≤ E [ f (ˆ x η )] -f ∗","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":225.60972628933723,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":155.15299999999996,"r":457.54451413,"t":243.81455570000003},"charspan":[0,99],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/81","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Invoking the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we obtain","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":203.10536100000002,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":316.59074927999984,"t":218.56355570000005},"charspan":[0,49],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/82","text":"Invoking the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we obtain"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"µ f 2 E [ ‖ ˆ x η -x ∗ ‖ 2 ] -‖∇ f ( x ∗ ) ‖ ∥ ∥ ∥ E [ ˆ x η -Π X ∗ h [ˆ x η ] ]∥ ∥ ∥ ≤ E [ f (ˆ x η )] -f ∗ .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":174.71843999999987,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":151.232,"r":461.9635479800001,"t":193.25255570000002},"charspan":[0,110],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/83","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Invoking the Jensen's inequality, we obtain","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":152.543361,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.00000000000011,"r":277.13035275999994,"t":168.00155569999993},"charspan":[0,43],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/84","text":"Invoking the Jensen's inequality, we obtain"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"µ f 2 E [ ‖ ˆ x η -x ∗ ‖ 2 ] -‖∇ f ( x ∗ ) ‖ E [∥ ∥ ∥ ˆ x η -Π X ∗ h [ˆ x η ] ∥ ∥ ∥ ] ≤ E [ f (ˆ x η )] -f ∗ .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":124.15643999999998,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":151.2320000000001,"r":461.9635479800001,"t":142.6905557},"charspan":[0,110],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/85","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Noting that E [dist(ˆ x η , X ∗ h )] = E [∥ ∥ ∥ ˆ x η -Π X ∗ h [ˆ x η ] ∥ ∥ ∥ ] , we obtain the lower bound in (i-2). (i-3) This result follows directly from (i-2).","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":82.38536099999988,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.72400000000013,"r":482.6134177099998,"t":113.26155569999992},"charspan":[0,164],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/86","text":"Noting that E [dist(ˆ x η , X ∗ h )] = E [∥ ∥ ∥ ˆ x η -Π X ∗ h [ˆ x η ] ∥ ∥ ∥ ] , we obtain the lower bound in (i-2). (i-3) This result follows directly from (i-2)."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(ii-1) This result is identical to (i-1).","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":68.8363609999999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.72400000000013,"r":245.18450902,"t":84.29455569999993},"charspan":[0,41],"pa